var tafsirData_79 = {
  "1": "\"By those ˹angels˺ stripping out ˹evil souls˺ harshly.\" opens Surah an-Nazi‘at with the angels who extract souls, the first of five oaths, mirroring winds sent, blowing, scattering, angels distinguishing, delivering.\n\n“By those angels stripping out evil souls harshly” translates “wa al-nazi‘ati gharqa,” by those who extract with violence, strongly, harshly, pulling evil souls as a thorned branch from wet wool, as with the raging Flame ripping scalps, the Fire that does not let live, the choking food, and the healer sought when soul reaches collar bones and no healer saves, the dying realizes it is time to depart, feet tied, driven to your Lord. Stripping harshly is for the transgressors, the wicked asked what landed you in Hell, not praying, not feeding poor, indulging, denying until inevitable came, whose home is endless ages with no coolness, boiling and pus.\n\nThis oath follows the imminent punishment and wishing to be dust, showing how dust-like death begins, and parallels the smooth earth lodging, mountains pegs, and the Day appointed.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think angels stripping harshly is cruelty without justice, like the brute’s snout marked. It is the fitting reward for the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, withholder, transgressor, brute who said ancient fables.\n\nFor the one created from humble fluid, stripping harshly should motivate being among those stripped gently next, the good souls pulled gently, by being among the excepted who pray consistently, give rightful share, believe firmly, fear punishment, guard chastity, keep trusts, and observe prayers, whose Gardens are held in honour and whose souls are drawn as scattered pearls, not harshly.\n\nFurther, stripping harshly also parallels the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, gossip-monger, withholder, transgressor, brute, whose wealth says ancient fables and whose snout will be marked. Harsh stripping is the fitting reward after never having faith in the Greatest nor encouraging feeding of the poor, where they will have no close friend here nor food except oozing pus which none eat except evildoers. To be stripped gently, be among those who give rightful share to the asker and deprived, who are properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour, where stripping is not harsh but the eternal youths as scattered pearls, and the bliss and vast kingdom await.",
  "2": "\"and those pulling out ˹good souls˺ gently.\" gives the counterpart to harsh stripping, the gentle extraction for the righteous.\n\n“and those pulling out good souls gently” translates “wa al-nashitati nash ta,” the active, to tie and untie gently, like the Pen writing truthfully and the well-preserved Record. Pulling gently indicates ease, as with the Garden’s shade right above, fruit easy, silver vessels precisely filled, ginger, Salsabil, and the pure drink from the Lord. Good souls, or the submitted not wicked, the people of the right, the righteous amid cool shade and springs, the virtuous who drink camphor, the excepted who pray, and the in-awe without seeing with mighty reward, are drawn gently, not ripped like scalps.\n\nThis follows stripping harshly to show two extractions, as with residents of Fire not equal residents of Paradise, and submitted not wicked.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think gentle pulling guarantees no accountability. Gentle is for those whose record will be “Read my record, I knew I would face my reckoning” in a life of bliss, but they still face presentation where no secret stays hidden.\n\nFor the living who were nothing, drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown grateful or ungrateful, the gentle pulling should make you choose grateful, fulfilling vows, feeding despite desire, so your soul is among the gently pulled, whose Gardens’ eternal youths scatter as pearls, bliss and vast kingdom, fine silk, silver bracelets, and pure drink await, not the raging Flame that summons hoarders who gathered and hoarded.\n\nMore: Pulling gently also mirrors the Pen writing truthfully and the well-preserved Record only purified touch, where pulling is precise. Gentle pulling indicates ease, as with the Garden’s shade right above, fruit easy, silver vessels precisely filled, ginger, Salsabil, and the pure drink from the Lord. Good souls pulled gently are the submitted not wicked, the people of the right, the righteous amid cool shade and springs, the virtuous who drink camphor, the excepted who pray, and the in-awe without seeing with mighty reward. To be pulled gently, be among the grateful who fulfil vows, fear sweeping horror, give food despite desire, and fear the distressful Day, so gentle is your soul’s extraction and your Gardens’ eternal youths, not harsh ripping.",
  "3": "\"and those gliding ˹through heavens˺ swiftly.\" continues the angels’ oaths after stripping and pulling, describing their movement through heavens.\n\n“and those gliding through heavens swiftly” translates “wa al-sabihati sabha,” the swimmers, gliders, as with the winds sent forth, blowing violently, scattering rainclouds, and the angels distinguishing and delivering. Gliding swiftly indicates the angels move through the seven mighty heavens without flaw where sight returns frustrated, the lowest heaven adorned with lamps as missiles, and the pathways of heavenly ascent where angels and the spirit ascend on a Day fifty thousand years.\n\nThis follows stripping and pulling to show that after souls are extracted, angels glide, and precedes taking the lead vigorously and conducting affairs obediently, showing hierarchy.\n\nGliding swiftly is the opposite of the garden owners’ fixated early proceeding and the despicable’s rushing to mock.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine angels gliding as mythic. Gliding is their obedience, as with the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey and the eight bearing the Throne.\n\nFor the believer who will take the path leading back to the Lord, gliding swiftly should inspire you to glide through night worship more impactful and day over-occupied duties with remembrance wholeheartedly, taking Trustee, so your gliding is smooth, not harsh, and your mustaqarr before your Lord is Gardens held in honour where you are told to eat and drink happily, not the smoke shade three columns neither cool nor shelter.\n\nExtra: Gliding swiftly also corresponds to the winds sent forth successively, blowing violently, scattering rainclouds widely, and the angels distinguishing fully and delivering revelation, ending excuses and giving warnings. Swift gliding through heavens shows the angels’ obedience, as with the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey and the eight bearing the Throne. Swift is not like the garden owners’ fixated early proceeding or the despicable’s rushing to mock, but is purposeful. For the one who loves fleeting and neglects weighty Day, gliding swiftly should inspire you to glide through night worship more impactful and day over-occupied duties with remembrance wholeheartedly, taking Trustee, so gliding is smooth, not harsh.",
  "4": "\"and those taking the lead vigorously.\" adds the fourth angelic oath after gliding. The angels race ahead.\n\n“and those taking the lead vigorously” translates “fa-al-sabiqati sabqa,” the preceders, racers, as with the foremost in faith will be foremost in Paradise, nearest to Allah, the competitors for forgiveness and Paradise as vast as heavens and earth, and the helpers who took the lead. Taking the lead vigorously indicates the angels hasten to execute command, as with the Pen, the Trumpet, and the Inevitable.\n\nThis follows gliding swiftly to show that gliding leads to leading.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think angels taking the lead competes with the Most Compassionate who holds all authority. Leading is obedience, like the eight bearing the Throne and the angels on sides of the torn sky.\n\nFor the one who loves fleeting and neglects weighty Day, taking the lead should be learned from them. Take the lead for the warning to humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag behind, every soul detained except people of the right, so lead becomes your Gardens where you ask about the wicked, not the lag of spooked zebras fleeing reminder and wishing for personal letters from the unseen Record to copy.\n\nFurther: Taking the lead vigorously also echoes the foremost in faith will be foremost in Paradise, nearest to Allah, the competitors for forgiveness and Paradise as vast as heavens and earth, and the helpers who took the lead. Vigorous leading indicates the angels hasten to execute command, as with the Pen, the Trumpet, and the Inevitable. Leading is obedience, like the eight bearing the Throne and the angels on sides of the torn sky. For the human created from humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, earth lodging, mountains pegs, taking the lead should be learned as well, to take the lead for the warning to humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, every soul detained except people of the right, so lead becomes your Gardens where you ask about the wicked, not the lag of spooked zebras.",
  "5": "\"and those conducting affairs ˹obediently˺\" completes the five oaths after stripping, pulling, gliding, and leading. The angels manage affairs.\n\n“and those conducting affairs obediently” translates “fa-al-mudabbirati amra,” the managers of command, as with the command descending between seven heavens so you may know He is Most Capable and encompasses all knowledge, the Pen and what everyone writes, the well-preserved Record, and the Lord of pathways where angels ascend.\n\nConducting affairs obediently indicates they follow the Master’s order, not their own, like the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey, the eight bearing the Throne, and the holy spirit and angels standing in ranks silent unless permitted.\n\nThis follows taking the lead vigorously to show that leading is for conducting.\n\nConducting affairs is not independent lordship, but tanfidh, as with the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak to Him, and the Day the holy spirit and angels stand in ranks.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to attribute lordship to angels, similar to seeking refuge with jinn, urging idols, or believing Allah has mate or offspring.\n\nFor the human created from humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, earth lodging, mountains pegs, the angels conducting affairs should make you take the Right Way while you will, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so conducting is for you Gardens held in honour where the Pen writes your success, not the raging Flame that summons hoarders.\n\nMore: Conducting affairs obediently also aligns with the command descending between seven heavens so you may know He is Most Capable and encompasses all knowledge, the Pen and what everyone writes, the well-preserved Record, and the Lord of pathways where angels ascend. Conducting affairs obediently indicates they follow the Master’s order, not their own, like the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey, the eight bearing the Throne, and the holy spirit and angels standing in ranks silent unless permitted. Conducting is not independent lordship, but tanfidh, as with the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak to Him. To be among those conducted to Gardens, be true to trusts and properly observant.",
  "6": "\"˹Consider˺ the Day ˹when˺ the quaking Blast will come to pass.\" shifts from angelic oaths to the Day they manage, the imminent punishment warned of, the Day every person will see what hands did and the disbeliever wishes to be dust, the Day they see as far but We see as near, the Day the soul reaches collar bones.\n\n“Consider the Day when the quaking Blast will come to pass” translates the first Blast, or “rajifah,” the trembling, quaking, as with the earth and mountains will shake violently, mountains become dunes, sky split apart, stars put out, sky torn, mountains blown to mirage, earth and mountains lifted and crushed with one blow, Trumpet blown, Inevitable has come to pass, and the challenging punishment bound to come. Quaking Blast is the same as the Trumpet’s one blast, the difficult Day far from easy, the Day the disbeliever wishes to be dust.\n\nThis follows conducting affairs to show what affairs they conduct toward, and precedes followed by a second Blast, hearts trembling, eyes downcast.\n\nThis Blast is the same as the stars put out, sky gates, mountains mirage, Hell ambush, but here described as quaking.\n\nFor the living who were told to make room, give rightful share, and walk upright, quaking Blast should move you to take the Right Way, recite what is easy, and be among the bright faces looking at their Lord, not the gloomy anticipating devastation, and among those who pray consistently, whose Gardens are held in honour where no quaking is, but fruit hangs within reach.\n\nExtra: Quaking Blast also is the Trumpet will be blown, and you will all come forth in crowds, the sky split open becoming many gates, the mountains blown away becoming mirage, Hell lying in ambush as home for transgressors where they remain for endless ages, no coolness or drink except boiling water and oozing pus, a fitting reward, never expecting reckoning, totally rejecting signs, precise recording, taste more torment, salvation for the righteous, Gardens, vineyards, full-bosomed maidens, full cups, never hearing idle talk, generous gift. Quaking Blast is the start of that appointed time that never delays, so consider it now, before the Day every person will see what hands did and the disbeliever wishes to be dust.",
  "7": "\"followed by a second Blast.\" gives the second quake after the first, the Trumpet’s pair. The two Blasts are the resurrection’s phases.\n\n“followed by a second Blast” translates “tatba‘uha al-radifah,” the follower follows, the second Blast follows the first, as with the Inevitable Event that will debase and elevate, the two woes and more woe, the two condemnations for the contemplator, and the Night Day cycle. Second Blast is the resurrection blast after the death blast, the one mighty Blast after which they will be above ground, as next states, “But indeed, it will take only one mighty Blast, and at once they will be above ground.”\n\nSecond Blast corresponds to the Trumpet’s second blowing, the sky torn, Throne borne, presentation, and the Gardens and Fire as homes.\n\nThis follows quaking Blast to show sequence, and precedes hearts trembling, eyes downcast, and the mocking “Will we really be restored to our former state even after reduced to decayed bones?”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to count Blasts as merely two, missing that the appointed time that can never be delayed includes both.\n\nFor the denier who asks “Will we really be restored after decayed bones? Such a return would be a total loss,” the second Blast answers that one mighty Blast suffices, and at once they will be above ground, so the appointed time’s appointed time is not a total loss for the mindful, but a total gain as Gardens held in honour where you are told to eat and drink joyfully, where the Pen has written your blast as bliss.\n\nFurther: Second Blast also follows the first quaking Blast as the second part of the Day fifty thousand years, the Inevitable Event that will debase and elevate, the two woes and more woe, the two condemnations for the contemplator, and the Night Day cycle. Second Blast is the resurrection blast after the death blast, the one mighty Blast after which they will be above ground, as next states, “But indeed, it will take only one mighty Blast, and at once they will be above ground.” It corresponds to the Trumpet’s second blowing, the sky torn, Throne borne, presentation, and the Gardens and Fire as homes. Second Blast is the mercy that brings the grateful to Gardens, not the woe to deniers.",
  "8": "\"˹The deniers'˺ hearts on that Day will be trembling ˹in horror˺\" describes the inner state when the quaking Blast comes and is followed by the second. Hearts tremble, not bodies only.\n\n“The deniers’ hearts on that Day will be trembling in horror” translates hearts quaking, as with the garden owners’ hearts when they saw devastation and said “We lost our way, we are deprived,” the hypocrites who thought every cry against them like planks, the despicable withholder whose snout will be marked, and the bright vs gloomy faces, anticipating devastating faqi rah. Trembling hearts, or “wājifah,” is to be agitated, as with the Fire that almost bursts, the raging Flame ripping scalps, and the dunes, the choking food.\n\nThis follows second Blast to show effect on deniers, and precedes eyes downcast, showing inner and outer.\n\nHearts trembling is the opposite of the self-reproaching soul that blames before and the in-awe without seeing with mighty reward, the right-hand’s “I knew I would face my reckoning” and its radiance and joy.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think trembling is mere fear of the quake, not of the reckoning. Trembling is horror at being presented with no secrets hidden and informed of what was sent forth.\n\nFor the living who are told to remember the Name morning and evening, prostrate part of night, glorify long, and love not fleeting but prepare for weighty Day ahead, trembling should be now as in awe, not then as horror, so your heart is not trembling in horror but in awe, and your face is bright looking at your Lord, where trembling is replaced by radiance and Gardens held in honour.\n\nMore: Trembling hearts on that Day also mirrors the garden owners’ hearts when they saw devastation and said “We lost our way, we are deprived,” the hypocrites who thought every cry against them like planks, the despicable withholder whose snout will be marked, and the bright vs gloomy faces anticipating faqi rah. Trembling hearts, or “wājifah,” is agitated, as with the Fire that almost bursts, the raging Flame ripping scalps, and the dunes. Hearts trembling is the opposite of the self-reproaching soul that blames before and the in-awe without seeing with mighty reward, the right-hand’s “I knew I would face my reckoning” and its radiance. To have a heart not trembling in horror, be in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, whose Gardens are held in honour.",
  "9": "\"with their eyes downcast.\" adds the outward sign to the trembling hearts on that Day. Eyes downcast shows humiliation.\n\n“with their eyes downcast” translates eyes humbled, as with eyes downcast covered with disgrace on the Day they come forth racing to an idol, eyes downcast totally covered with disgrace when Shin bared and asked to prostrate but cannot, faces gloomy anticipating devastation, woe and more woe. Downcast, or “khashi‘ah,” is humbled, as with the sight returning frustrated and weary after looking again at flawless heavens, now humbled not by flaw but by horror.\n\nThis follows hearts trembling to show inner fear appears outward, and precedes “But now they ask mockingly, ‘Will we really be restored...’” showing that the same ones who mocked now have downcast eyes.\n\nEyes downcast contrasts with bright faces looking at their Lord.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to see downcast eyes as modest piety, like the wrapped and covered in humility. Downcast here is disgrace, not devotion, as with the snout marked.\n\nFor practice, lower your eyes now in humility before the Lord of east and west, take Him as Trustee, recite what is easy, and walk spacious pathways with purpose, so downcast on that Day is not yours, but bright is, and your heart trembles in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness, not the trembling horror of the deniers’ hearts on the Day when the quaking and second Blast come.\n\nExtra: Eyes downcast also shows humiliation, as with eyes downcast covered with disgrace on the Day they come forth racing to an idol, eyes downcast totally covered with disgrace when Shin bared and asked to prostrate but cannot, faces gloomy anticipating devastation, woe and more woe. Downcast, or “khashi‘ah,” is humbled, as with the sight returning frustrated and weary after looking again at flawless heavens, now humbled not by flaw but by horror. Downcast contrasts with bright faces looking at their Lord. To have eyes not downcast in disgrace, lower your eyes now in humility before the Lord of east and west, take Him as Trustee, recite what is easy, and walk spacious pathways with purpose, so downcast on that Day is not yours, but bright is, and your heart trembles in awe without seeing, not horror.",
  "10": "\"˹But now˺ they ask ˹mockingly˺, \"Will we really be restored to our former state.\" quotes the deniers’ mocking question before the quake, when they are still secure, not yet trembling. The mock is about restoration.\n\n“But now they ask mockingly, ‘Will we really be restored to our former state’” translates the deniers’ question when they are still in the world, before the quaking and second Blast, when they feel secure that One in heaven will not swallow. Will we really be restored to our former state, or “a-inna la-marduduna fi al-hafirah,” will we be returned to the pit, to the first life, as with “Were we not once a sperm-drop? Then a clinging clot, then perfected, produced both sexes, Is such a Creator unable?” and “Do people think We cannot reassemble bones? Yes, capable of fingertips,” and “Do you see any of them left alive?” after ‘Ad. Restored to former state is resurrection they denied, as with “We will not be resurrected. Yes, by your Lord you will surely be resurrected and that is easy,” and the garden’s better garden hoped for.\n\nMockingly indicates the question is not sincere like “which powerless force will help?” but taunting, as with “When will this threat come if you are truthful?” and “How can humans be guides?” and “Ancient fables.”\n\nThis follows eyes downcast to contrast present mocking with future humiliation, and precedes even after reduced to decayed bones, adding mocking detail.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as ancient Meccans only. Mocking restoration is also loving fleeting and neglecting weighty Day, withholding good, and feeling secure.\n\nFor the living, do not ask mockingly. Ask to prepare, as with “consider if your water were to sink, who will bring flowing water?” and “Were they not once a sperm-drop?” Those questions lead to awe, walking upright, and taking the Right Way, so restoration to former state is not mocked loss but the better garden, the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, and the mustaqarr before your Lord is Gardens held in honour.",
  "11": "\"even after we have been reduced to decayed bones?\"\" intensifies the mock after “Will we really be restored to our former state?” The mock adds the decayed state.\n\n“even after we have been reduced to decayed bones?” translates “a-idha kunna ‘izaman nakhirah,” when we are crumbled, decayed bones, hollow, like the mountains blown to mirage, earth and mountains crushed, garden reduced to ashes, and ‘Ad like uprooted palm trunks, and “Do you see any left alive?” Decayed bones, or “nakhirah,” is rotten, crumbly, as with the humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, now decayed.\n\nEven after we have been reduced shows that the mockers think decay makes resurrection impossible, like the garden owners thought “we lost our way” was merely misdirection, not deprivation, and like those who felt secure that earth will not swallow.\n\nThis follows former state to show that the mockers add the worst case, decayed, to argue impossibility, similar to the challenge demanding punishment bound to come.\n\nAdding, “Then such a return would be a total loss for us! But indeed, it will take only one mighty Blast, and at once they will be above ground,” next, shows their calculation that return would be loss, like the wicked who said woe and more woe and were told how evil was what they determined.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to accept decay as proof against resurrection, as with “We created you yet some are disbelievers, He is All-Seeing,” and “He created heavens and earth for a purpose, shaped you perfecting form, to Him is final return.”\n\nFor the believer who remembers the period when they were nothing worth mentioning, then a drop, then hearing and seeing, Way shown grateful or ungrateful, decayed bones is not an argument but a reminder that the One who created seven heavens without flaw and smooths earth can reassemble bones and fingertips, so mocking becomes the bright faces looking at their Lord, not gloomy anticipating devastation, and returning is not a total loss but the ultimate truth that you are told to eat and drink joyfully.",
  "12": "\"Adding, \"Then such a return would be a ˹total˺ loss ˹for us˺!\"\" completes the deniers’ mocking calculation after former state and decayed bones. The loss is their evaluation.\n\n“Adding, ‘Then such a return would be a total loss for us!’” translates “qalu tilka idhan karratun khasirah,” then that return would be a losing return, a total loss, as with the true losers who let wealth or children divert from remembrance, the garden owners who said “We have been deprived” and “We have certainly been transgressors” after woe, and the disbelievers who will cry “Oh, I wish I were dust!” on the Day every person will see what hands did. Total loss, or “khasirah,” is the same loss as the five under: chains, shackles, blazing Fire, and the endless ages, not tasting coolness.\n\nThen such a return would be a total loss is their sarcasm, meaning if we are to be restored after decayed bones, that return is bad for us, we would rather remain dust, similar to “I wish death was the end!” and “nor known my reckoning, would that it had been decisive.”\n\nThis follows decayed bones to show that the mockers see resurrection not as hope but as loss, because their Way was ungrateful, indulging, amusing until they face their Day, and their wealth and children only increased them in loss.\n\nBut indeed, it will take only one mighty Blast, and at once they will be above ground, next, shows that the return will happen despite their evaluation, and it will be easy for Allah who is Most Capable.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear total loss as economic, like the caravan fanfare better than what is with Allah. Total loss here is the recompense for never expecting reckoning, totally rejecting signs, and everything recorded precisely, so taste more torment, while salvation is for the righteous who will have Gardens, vineyards, maidens, full cups, never hearing idle talk, a generous gift.\n\nFor the living, do not calculate return as loss. Calculate it as the grateful do, fulfilling vows, giving food despite desire, fearing the sweeping horror, so return is not khasirah but mafaza, salvation, Gardens held in honour where you are told to eat and drink happily for what you used to do, and your striving has been appreciated.",
  "13": "\"But indeed, it will take only one ˹mighty˺ Blast.\" answers the mock “Such a return would be a total loss for us!” with ease. One mighty Blast is enough.\n\n“But indeed, it will take only one mighty Blast” translates “fa-innama hiya zajratun wahidah,” then indeed it is a single cry, a harsh shout, as with the Trumpet will be blown with one blast and earth and mountains lifted and crushed with one blow, the Inevitable that will have come to pass, and the Day the Trumpet will be sounded as difficult Day. One mighty Blast, or “zajratun wahidah,” is a single, powerful cry, not successive winds or successive angels, but one, like the Pen’s one writing and the well-preserved Record.\n\nBut indeed, or emphatic, shows that the deniers’ calculation of total loss does not prevent the Blast, just as the garden reduced to ashes while they slept was one visitor, and the blast for Thamud was one overwhelming, and the wind for ‘Ad seven nights, all ways of Our punishment in this world.\n\nOne mighty Blast is easy for the One who created seven heavens one above another without flaw, made the Pen, and is Most Capable of everything, Most Capable of restoring fingertips, and Lord of easts and wests truly capable to replace and cannot be prevented.\n\nThis follows total loss to show that loss will come regardless of their evaluation, and precedes and at once they will be above ground, showing immediacy.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think one Blast minimizes resurrection’s greatness, as with ancient fables dismissal. One Blast shows greatness of the Blower, not belittlement of the Day.\n\nFor the believer who was nothing, then a drop to test, shown the Way, grateful, the one mighty Blast is not total loss but total gain, as with the Garden under rivers for ever and ever, that is ultimate triumph, where one blast becomes your “Read my record” in a life of bliss, and the appointed time that can never be delayed becomes your mustaqarr before your Lord, where bright faces look at Him.",
  "14": "\"and at once they will be above ground.\" completes the one mighty Blast’s effect. The emergence is instantaneous.\n\n“and at once they will be above ground” translates “fa-idha hum bi-s-sahirah,” then they are on the awakened surface, the open expanse, as with the Day they will come forth from the graves swiftly, as if racing to an idol, with eyes downcast, utterly covered with disgrace, That is the Day they have always been warned of, and the Day every soul will be detained except the people of the right in Gardens asking about the wicked. Sahirah is the level, wakeful plain, opposite to the earth smoothed like a bed and lodging for the living and the dead, now the surface where they stand, similar to the dunes of shifting sand but now flat.\n\nAt once, or “fa-idha,” indicates immediacy after one Blast, as with the garden reduced to ashes while they slept and at daybreak they called, but here at once after the Blast they are above ground, not needing seven nights like ‘Ad.\n\nThis follows one mighty Blast to show that Blast’s result is above ground, not in graves, and precedes Has the story of Moses reached you, giving historical example of a messenger before that Day.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to picture above ground as mere physical relocation, not as presentation where no secret stays hidden and all will be informed of what was sent forth and left behind, despite excuses, the soul testifying against itself.\n\nFor the living who are told to arise and warn, being above ground then should motivate arising now, revering your Lord, purifying garments, shunning idols, not expecting more, and persevering, so at once above ground is not the trembling hearts and downcast eyes of deniers, but the bright faces looking at their Lord, the radiance and joy of those delivered from horror and granted silk, reclining on couches never seeing scorching heat, where the one mighty Blast is your single call to Gardens held in honour.",
  "15": "“Has the story of Moses reached you ˹O Prophet˺?” opens the account of Moses with a question that summons the hearer, and the surah turns from the oaths about the angels of death to the story that gives the warning its shape. “Has the story of Moses reached you O Prophet?” or “hal ataka hadeethu Moosa.” Ibn Kathir explains that the question is the Quran's way of drawing the listener into the story, and the believer is taught that the story of Moses is the story that the Prophet is asked to recall, and that the recalling is for the sake of the lesson, and that the story is not told for the memory of Egypt but for the warning of the present, and that the believer is taught to hear the story as the story of every prophet and every tyrant. Al-Tabari notes that the question is rhetorical, for the story has indeed reached the Prophet, and the believer is taught that the question is the invitation to reflect on what has reached, and that the reflection is the purpose of the reaching.\n\nAl-Razi observes the function of the question, for the verse teaches the believer the manner of the Quran in telling the histories, and that the histories are told with the question that awakens, and that the believer is taught to approach the stories with the ear of the one who is asked and not the eye of the one who passes, and to know that the story which is received with the attention is the story which does its work. The verse also teaches the believer the centrality of the story of Moses, for the story of Moses and Pharaoh is the story that the Quran returns to again and again, and the believer is taught that the returning is the sign of the story's weight, and that the story gathers every lesson of the struggle between the truth and the power, and that the believer is taught to read it as the model of his own struggle. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive the story of Moses as the story addressed to him, and to know that the question “has the story reached you” is the question that the believer answers with his attention and his lesson.",
  "16": "“His Lord called him in the sacred valley of Ṭuwa.” records the beginning of Moses' mission, and the verse names the place where the Lord called him. “His Lord called him in the sacred valley of Tuwa,” or “idh nadahu rabbuhu bil-wadi al-muqaddasi Tuwa.” Ibn Kathir explains that God called Moses in the sacred valley of Tuwa, and the believer is taught that the calling of Moses was the beginning of his prophethood, and that the place of the calling was made sacred by the calling, and that the Lord who called Moses in the valley is the Lord who calls every prophet at the place He wills, and that the believer is taught to honor the places where the Lord calls and the times when He calls. Al-Tabari notes the word “muqaddas” — “sacred,” “purified” — and the believer is taught that the valley was sanctified by the presence of the Lord, and that the sanctity of the place is the sanctity of the One who spoke there, and that the believer is taught to treat the signs of the Lord with the reverence of the place.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the calling, for the verse teaches the believer that the prophet is the one whom the Lord calls, and that the calling is the mark of the prophethood, and that the one who is called is the one who is sent, and that the believer is taught to answer the call of the Lord through the call of the messenger, and to know that the call which began in the valley of Tuwa is the call which continues through the message, and that the believer is taught to hear the call in the message. The verse also teaches the believer the humility of the beginning, for Moses was called in a valley and not in a palace, and the believer is taught that the Lord chooses the humble places and the humble servants, and that the honor of Moses was the honor of the calling and not the honor of the place, and that the believer is taught to seek the calling and not the place. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to honor the beginning of the mission, and to know that the sacred valley is the sign of the sacred message, and that the believer is taught to stand in the valley with Moses and to hear the call.",
  "17": "“˹commanding,˺ ’Go to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed ˹all bounds˺” records the command that the calling carried, and the verse gives the first words of the mission. “Commanding, 'Go to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed all bounds,'” or “ithhab ila Fir'awna innahu tagha.” Ibn Kathir explains that God commanded Moses to go to Pharaoh because Pharaoh had transgressed, and the believer is taught that the mission of the prophet begins with the going to the transgressor, and that the prophet is sent to the one who has exceeded the bounds, and that the sending is the mercy to the transgressor before it is the warning, and that the believer is taught to carry the truth to the transgressor and not to flee from him. Al-Tabari notes the word “tagha” — “transgressed,” “exceeded” — and the believer is taught that the transgression of Pharaoh was the transgression of the one who claimed the lordship and oppressed the servants, and that the transgression is the cause of the sending, and that the believer is taught to name the transgression and to confront it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the going, for the verse teaches the believer that the truth does not avoid the tyrant but goes to him, and that the messenger is sent to the palace and not only to the tent, and that the believer is taught to speak the truth to the powerful and not to reserve it for the weak, and to know that the going to the transgressor is the first step of the mission, and that the one who goes has done the first duty. The verse also teaches the believer the diagnosis of the transgression, for the Lord who sent Moses named the disease before the remedy, and the believer is taught to name the wrong before he calls to the right, and to know that the naming of the transgression is the beginning of the cure, and that the messenger who goes to Pharaoh is the messenger who has named what he goes to oppose. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to go to the transgressor with the truth, and to know that the transgression of Pharaoh is the transgression of every tyrant, and that the believer is taught to be the Moses of his own time, going to the transgression with the call.",
  "18": "“And say, 'Would you ˹be willing to˺ purify yourself.” records the first words that Moses was commanded to speak, and the verse shows the gentleness with which the messenger was to address the tyrant. “And say, 'Would you be willing to purify yourself,'” or “fa-qul hal laka ila an tazakka.” Ibn Kathir explains that Moses was commanded to ask Pharaoh whether he would purify himself, and the believer is taught that the call to the tyrant begins with the call to the purification, and that the purification is the leaving of the sin and the turning to the Lord, and that the messenger offers the purification before he threatens the punishment, and that the believer is taught to call to the purification with the gentleness of the question and not the harshness of the command. Al-Tabari notes the word “tazakka” — “purify yourself” — and the believer is taught that the purification is the cleansing of the heart and the deed, and that the one who is offered the purification is offered the whole of the mercy, and that the believer is taught to offer the purification to the sinner and not to despair of him.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the gentleness, for the verse teaches the believer the manner of the call to the powerful, and that the call to the tyrant is the call of the gentle question, and that the gentleness is the wisdom, and that the one who calls with the harshness has closed the door that the gentleness would open, and that the believer is taught to call with the gentleness that the verse models, and to know that the gentleness of Moses to Pharaoh was the gentleness of the Lord who sent him. The verse also teaches the believer the priority of the purification, for the first call is the call to the purity and not the call to the politics, and the believer is taught to put the purification first, and to know that the purification of the self is the door of every reform, and that the one who is purified is the one who is saved. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to call to the purification with the gentleness, and to know that the question “would you purify yourself” is the question that the believer is taught to ask, and that the asking is the first step of the mercy.",
  "19": "“and let me guide you to your Lord so that you will be in awe ˹of Him˺?'’” completes the first address of Moses, and the verse gives the guidance that the purification would open. “And let me guide you to your Lord so that you will be in awe of Him,” or “wa ahdiyaka ila rabbika fa-takhsha.” Ibn Kathir explains that Moses offered to guide Pharaoh to his Lord so that Pharaoh might fear Him, and the believer is taught that the guidance to the Lord is the purpose of the message, and that the fear of the Lord is the fruit of the guidance, and that the one who is guided to the Lord is the one who fears Him, and that the believer is taught to offer the guidance before the warning, and to know that the guidance is the mercy and the warning is the consequence. Al-Tabari notes the word “takhsha” — “you fear,” “you stand in awe” — and the believer is taught that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of the wisdom, and that the one who is guided to the fear is the one who has been guided to the whole of the good.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the offer, for the verse teaches the believer that the messenger offers the guidance and not the compulsion, and that the offer is the mark of the mercy, and that the one who is offered the guidance and refuses has refused the mercy, and that the believer is taught to offer the guidance and to leave the choice, and to know that the guidance is the gift that the messenger carries and the choice is the response that the hearer makes. The verse also teaches the believer the goal of the guidance, for the guidance is to the Lord and to the fear of the Lord, and the believer is taught to seek the guidance that leads to the fear, and to know that the guidance which does not produce the fear is the guidance which has not reached the heart, and that the believer is taught to let the guidance reach his heart and to let the fear follow. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to offer the guidance and to seek the fear, and to know that the one who is guided to the Lord is the one who is saved from the fate of Pharaoh, and that the believer is taught to accept the guidance and to live in the fear.",
  "20": "“Then Moses showed him the great sign.” records the sign that Moses showed, and the verse marks the turn from the call to the proof. “Then Moses showed him the great sign,” or “fa-arahul-ayata al-kubra.” Ibn Kathir explains that Moses showed Pharaoh the great sign — the staff that became a serpent, and the commentators mention the staff and the hand — and the believer is taught that the messenger accompanies the call with the proof, and that the proof of Moses was the great sign that left Pharaoh without excuse, and that the believer is taught to ground his call in the proof, and to know that the truth which is supported by the sign is the truth which the rejecter cannot answer. Al-Tabari notes the word “kubra” — “the great,” “the greatest” — and the believer is taught that the sign of Moses was the great sign, and that the greatness of the sign is the measure of the mercy, and that the Lord who sent the great sign is the Lord who desired the turning of Pharaoh.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the sign, for the verse teaches the believer that the sign of God is the mercy that precedes the punishment, and that the great sign was shown to Pharaoh so that he might believe, and that the sign which is shown and refused becomes the proof against the refuser, and that the believer is taught to receive the signs of God as the mercies they are, and to know that the sign which is refused in this world is the witness in the next. The verse also teaches the believer the order of the mission, for the call came first and the sign second, and the believer is taught to call and then to prove, and to know that the sign which follows the call is the sign that confirms the call, and that the messenger who calls and proves has done the whole of his duty. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the signs of God as the mercies of God, and to know that the great sign of Moses is the sign of every messenger, and that the believer is taught to believe the sign and to be saved.",
  "21": "“but he denied and disobeyed ˹Allah˺,” records the response of Pharaoh to the sign, and the verse states the refusal in its two parts. “But he denied and disobeyed Allah,” or “fa-kaththaba wa 'asa.” Ibn Kathir explains that Pharaoh denied the sign and disobeyed the command, and the believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the denial of the proof and the disobedience of the command, and that the two are the two halves of the one refusal, and that the one who denies the sign has disobeyed the Lord of the sign, and that the believer is taught to fear the refusal that denies and disobeys. Al-Tabari notes the two words — “kaththaba” and “'asa” — and the believer is taught that the denial is the refusal of the heart and the disobedience is the refusal of the limbs, and that the two refusals are the complete rejection, and that the believer is taught to accept with the heart and to obey with the limbs.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the two refusals, for the verse teaches the believer that the rejection of the truth is never half, and that the one who denies the proof will disobey the command, and that the denial and the disobedience are the two steps of the one descent, and that the believer is taught to stop the descent at the first step, and to know that the one who denies the sign has already disobeyed the Lord, and that the disobedience is the fruit of the denial. The verse also teaches the believer the guilt of Pharaoh, for the sign was great and the refusal was complete, and the believer is taught that the greatness of the sign deepens the guilt of the refusal, and that the one who is given the great sign and refuses has refused the great mercy, and that the believer is taught to answer the great mercy with the great acceptance. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive the signs with the acceptance and the obedience, and to know that the one who denies and disobeys is the one whose next step is the turning away that the next verse records.",
  "22": "“then turned his back, striving ˹against the truth˺.” records the next step of Pharaoh's rejection, and the verse shows the tyrant turning from the truth to the struggle against it. “Then turned his back, striving against the truth,” or “thumma adbara yas'a.” Ibn Kathir explains that Pharaoh turned his back and strove against the truth, and the believer is taught that the rejection of the truth passes from the refusal to the striving, and that the one who refuses the truth becomes the one who fights it, and that the striving against the truth is the striving against the Lord of the truth, and that the believer is taught to recognize the descent of the rejecter and to fear it. Al-Tabari notes the word “yas'a” — “he strives,” “he works” — and the believer is taught that the striving of Pharaoh was the striving of the one who had chosen the opposition, and that the striving against the truth is the work of the one whose refusal has become the cause, and that the believer is taught to strive for the truth and not against it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the striving, for the verse teaches the believer that the truth is met with the striving, and that the rejecter does not rest in his rejection but works against the truth, and that the believer is taught to expect the striving and to meet it with the striving for the truth, and to know that the striving against the truth is the striving that fails, and that the one who strives against the Lord of the truth is the one who strives against the impossible. The verse also teaches the believer the completeness of the opposition, for Pharaoh turned his back and strove, and the believer is taught that the turning and the striving are the two acts of the enemy, and that the believer is taught to turn to the truth and to strive for it, and to know that the direction of the striving is the difference between the saved and the lost. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to strive for the truth and to know that the one who strives against it is the one whose striving the next verses answer.",
  "23": "“Then he summoned ˹his people˺ and called out,” records the climax of Pharaoh's rebellion, and the verse shows the tyrant gathering his people for the claim that would seal his ruin. “Then he summoned his people and called out,” or “fa-hashara fa-nada.” Ibn Kathir explains that Pharaoh gathered his people and called out to them, and the believer is taught that the tyrant strengthens his rebellion by the gathering of the crowd, and that the call of the tyrant is the call to the assembly, and that the believer is taught to see the gathering of the tyrant as the gathering of the doomed, and to know that the assembly which is gathered for the falsehood is the assembly which is scattered by the truth. Al-Tabari notes the word “hashara” — “he gathered” — and the believer is taught that the gathering of Pharaoh was the gathering of his power, and that the power which is gathered for the defiance is the power which the decree scatters.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the gathering, for the verse teaches the believer that the tyrant needs the crowd, and that the claim of the tyrant is the claim which is made to the crowd and for the crowd, and that the believer is taught to see through the gathering, and to know that the crowd which hears the claim of the tyrant is the crowd which is responsible for the acceptance or the refusal of the claim, and that the believer is taught to refuse the claim and to leave the crowd of the tyrant. The verse also teaches the believer the manner of the tyrant, for the tyrant calls out to his people and makes his claim in the open, and the believer is taught to answer the claim in the open, and to know that the truth is not silenced by the gathering, and that the messenger who spoke to the tyrant alone is the messenger who speaks to the truth in every assembly. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to recognize the gathering of the tyrant and to refuse it, and to know that the claim which the tyrant makes to the crowd is the claim which the next verse records and the decree answers.",
  "24": "“saying, ’I am your lord, the most high!’” records the claim that Pharaoh made to his people, and the verse preserves the boast that is the summit of the human rebellion. “Saying, 'I am your lord, the most high!'” or “fa-qala ana rabbukumul-a'la.” Ibn Kathir explains that Pharaoh said to his people, “I am your lord, the most high,” and the believer is taught that this claim is the summit of the transgression, and that the one who claims the lordship has claimed the place of God, and that the claim is the sin that the surah has been building toward, and that the believer is taught to see in the claim of Pharaoh the claim of every tyrant, and to know that the claim of the lordship is the claim that the Lord of the lords answers. Al-Tabari notes the word “al-a'la” — “the most high” — and the believer is taught that Pharaoh claimed the height that belongs to God alone, and that the claim of the height is the claim of the lordship, and that the believer is taught to give the height to the One who deserves it and to refuse it to the one who claims it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the claim, for the verse teaches the believer the end of the arrogance, and that the arrogance which begins with the wealth and the power ends with the claim of the divinity, and that the believer is taught to watch the first step of the arrogance, and to know that the one who is not humbled before God will end by claiming the place of God, and that the claim of Pharaoh is the warning of the end of every arrogance. The verse also teaches the believer the answer to the claim, for the claim of Pharaoh is answered by the drowning of Pharaoh, and the believer is taught that the Lord who is the most high is the Lord who lowers the claimant, and that the believer is taught to humble himself before the most high, and to know that the one who humbles himself is raised and the one who raises himself is lowered. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the claim of the self, and to know that the one who claims the place of God is the one whom the decree of God overtakes, and that the believer is taught to say of God what Pharaoh said of himself, and to give the height to the Lord alone.",
  "25": "“So Allah overtook him, making him an example in this life and the next.” records the answer to the claim, and the verse states the punishment that the boasting earned. “So Allah overtook him, making him an example in this life and the next,” or “fa-akhathahu Allahu nakala al-akhirati wal-ula.” Ibn Kathir explains that God seized Pharaoh with the punishment, and the believer is taught that the claim of the lordship was answered by the seizure of the Lord, and that the one who claimed the height was lowered to the sea, and that the punishment of Pharaoh was made an example — “nakal” — in this world and the next, and that the believer is taught that the example of Pharaoh is the warning of every claimant, and that the one who reads the example is the one who is spared the fate. Al-Tabari notes the word “nakal” — “example,” “warning punishment” — and the believer is taught that the punishment of Pharaoh was made the standing example, and that the example is for the first life and the last, and that the believer is taught to read the example in both.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the example, for the verse teaches the believer that the punishment of the tyrant is the lesson of the ages, and that the example of Pharaoh is the example that the Quran preserves, and that the believer is taught to learn from the example and not to repeat the claim, and to know that the one who repeats the claim repeats the fate, and that the example is the mercy to the one who will learn. The verse also teaches the believer the two-fold punishment, for the example is in this life and the next, and the believer is taught that the punishment of the tyrant is not ended by the drowning, and that the Hereafter holds the punishment that the sea only began, and that the believer is taught to fear the two punishments and to flee the claim that earns them. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the example of Pharaoh with the fear, and to know that the one who is made an example is the one whose fate is the warning, and that the believer is taught to be the one who learns the example and not the one who is made the example.",
  "26": "“Surely in this is a lesson for whoever stands in awe of ˹Allah˺.” closes the account with the lesson that the story was told to teach, and the verse states the audience of the example. “Surely in this is a lesson for whoever stands in awe of Allah,” or “inna fee thalika la-'ibratan li-man yakhsha.” Ibn Kathir explains that the story of Moses and Pharaoh is a lesson for the one who fears God, and the believer is taught that the lesson of the story profits the one who fears, and that the fear is the condition of the profit, and that the one who hears the story without the fear has heard the sound and missed the lesson, and that the believer is taught to hear the story with the fear, and to know that the fear is the beginning of the wisdom. Al-Tabari notes the word “'ibrah” — “lesson,” “warning” — and the believer is taught that the story is the warning of the one who fears, and that the warning is the mercy of the one who learns, and that the believer is taught to let the story warn him.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the condition, for the verse teaches the believer that the stories of the Quran are not told for the entertainment but for the lesson, and that the lesson is received by the one who fears, and that the believer is taught to cultivate the fear of God so that the stories may do their work in him, and to know that the one who hears the story of Pharaoh and does not fear has heard the story of another and not his own. The verse also teaches the believer the connection between the fear and the lesson, for the fear opens the heart to the lesson, and the lesson deepens the fear, and the believer is taught to let the two strengthen each other, and to know that the one who fears is the one who learns, and the one who learns is the one who is saved. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the stories of the Quran with the fear of God, and to know that the lesson of Moses and Pharaoh is the lesson of the believer's own struggle, and that the believer is taught to be of those who stand in awe, and to take the lesson that the awe opens.",
  "27": "\"Which is harder to create: you or the sky? He built it,\" asks after the lesson for whoever stands in awe, comparing creation difficulties to prove resurrection.\n\n“Which is harder to create: you or the sky? He built it” translates the challenge, as with “Is such a Creator unable to bring the dead back to life?” after sperm-drop to clot to male and female, and “Do people think We cannot reassemble bones? Yes, capable of fingertips,” and “Were they not once a sperm-drop emitted? Then a clinging clot, then He developed and perfected.” Which is harder humbles the denier who mocks “Will we really be restored after decayed bones? Such a return would be a total loss!” and the challenger demanding punishment.\n\nYou or the sky contrasts the small, despised fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, with the seven mighty heavens built above you without flaw where sight returns frustrated, the sky split open becoming many gates, the sky torn frail with angels on sides, eight bearing Throne, sky like molten brass, and the sky that will even turn children’s hair grey, His promise must be fulfilled.\n\nHe built it indicates the Builder is Allah, Lord of heavens and earth, Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak, the Blessed in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable, who created death and life to test.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to debate which is harder as if creation has difficulty for Allah. Harder is for human imagination, not for the Most Capable for whom reassembling bones and fingertips is easy.\n\nFor the one who was nothing worth mentioning, then a drop for test, shown grateful or ungrateful, which is harder should make you answer with the next verses, raising it high, forming it flawlessly, dimming night, bringing forth daylight, spreading earth, bringing forth water and pastures, setting mountains, all as sustenance, so the sky you see torn as gates becomes your Gardens’ vast kingdom, not your woe.",
  "28": "\"raising it high and forming it flawlessly,\" details building the sky after asking which is harder, you or the sky. The building is perfect.\n\n“raising it high and forming it flawlessly” translates “rafa‘a samkaha fa-sawwaha,” He raised its height and proportioned it, as with He shaped you perfecting form, He developed and perfected, He created seven heavens one above another without flaw where sight returns frustrated, and the Pen. Raising high, or “rafa‘a samkaha,” is to lift, as with earth and mountains lifted and crushed, Throne borne above. Forming flawlessly, or “sawwaha,” is to proportion, to perfect, like the Garden and silk, the crystalline silver filled precisely, and the appointed time perfectly ordained, How excellent are We in doing so.\n\nThis follows which is harder to show how sky was built.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think flawless forming means sky is static, not that it will be split open, become many gates, torn frail, like molten brass.\n\nFor the living who love fleeting and neglect weighty Day, raising high and forming flawlessly should inspire awe of Majesty, not secure feeling that One in heaven will not cause earth to swallow. Let flawless forming lead you to take the Right Way, recite what is easy, and be among the bright faces looking at their Lord, where raising high becomes your elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, not the smoke shade three columns neither cool nor shelter.\n\nMore: Raising it high and forming it flawlessly also details the sky’s building after asking which is harder, you or the sky, He built it. Raising high, or “rafa‘a samkaha,” is to lift, as with earth and mountains lifted and crushed, Throne borne above. Forming flawlessly, or “sawwaha,” is to proportion, like He shaped you perfecting form, the Garden and silk, the crystalline silver filled precisely, and the appointed time perfectly ordained. To see raising high and forming flawlessly as excellent, be grateful, show Way, choose grateful, fulfil vows, give food despite desire, so flawless forming becomes your Gardens held in honour where fruit is easy, shade above, silver vessels precisely filled, and ginger from Salsabil, all perfectly ordained as Gardens, not chains.",
  "29": "\"He dimmed its night, and brought forth its daylight,\" continues the sky building after raising high and forming flawlessly. Night and day are from the sky.\n\n“He dimmed its night, and brought forth its daylight” translates “wa aghtasha laylaha wa akhraja duhaha,” He darkened its night and brought out its forenoon light, as with the night as it retreats, the day as it breaks, Hell is mightiest catastrophe, a warning to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, and the night worship more impactful and day over-occupied, the Lord of east and west, no god except Him, take Him as Trustee.\n\nDimmed its night, or “aghtasha,” is to darken, as with the moon dimmed and sun and moon brought together when sight is stunned, and the night as cover, sleep for rest, where the wrapped stands.\n\nBrought forth its daylight, or “akhraja duhaha,” is to bring out its brightness, as with the shining lamp, the radiant lamp within seven heavens, and the day for livelihood, moving about smoothed earth.\n\nThis follows raising high to show that the sky’s height includes night and day.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to take dimming night and bringing daylight as mere astronomy, not as mercy and test, like the pen and the reminder.\n\nFor the human created from humble fluid, dimming night and bringing daylight should make you be in awe, fulfill vows, give food despite desire, so dimmed night becomes your night more impactful for recitation and brought forth daylight becomes your day for livelihood with remembrance often to be successful, where the weighty Day ahead of them is not neglected but prepared for with Gardens held in honour where you never see scorching heat or bitter cold.\n\nExtra: Dimmed its night and brought forth its daylight also continues the sky building after raising high and forming flawlessly. Night and day are from the sky, as with the night as it retreats, the day as it breaks, Hell is mightiest catastrophe, a warning to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag, and the night worship more impactful and day over-occupied. Dimmed its night, or “aghtasha,” is to darken, as with the moon dimmed and sun and moon brought together when sight is stunned, and the night as cover, sleep for rest, where the wrapped stands. Bringing forth daylight is to bring out brightness, as with the shining lamp, the radiant lamp. For the human created from humble fluid, dimmed night and brought forth daylight should make you be in awe, fulfil vows, give food despite desire, so dimmed night becomes your night more impactful for recitation and brought forth daylight becomes your day for livelihood with remembrance often to be successful.",
  "30": "\"As for the earth, He spread it out as well,\" shifts from sky building to earth spreading after dimming night and bringing daylight. The earth is spread.\n\n“As for the earth, He spread it out as well” translates “wa al-arda ba‘da dhalika dahaha,” and the earth, after that, He spread, as with the earth a lodging for the living and the dead, the earth smoothed like a bed, pegs, pairs, sleep, night, day, seven heavens, lamp, rain, grain, dense orchards, appointed Day, and the earth smoothed for you to move about and eat from provision and to Him is resurrection, and the earth spread for spacious pathways to walk. Spread, or “dahaha,” is to spread, to smooth, to extend, like the garden’s spread, the Pen’s spread, and the Reminder spread to the whole world.\n\nAs well indicates after building sky, He spread earth, showing the same Builder does both, the One - asked which is harder, you or the sky, He built it—can restore.\n\nThis follows dimming night and daylight to show sky and earth both prepared.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to debate earth shape, missing that spread is about usability, lodging, walking, as with dense orchards, not about denying seven heavens.\n\nFor the one who asks mockingly “Will we be restored after decayed bones? Such a return would be a total loss!” spreading should answer, as with then they became a clinging clot, produced both sexes, Is such a Creator unable? Spread earth will be lifted and crushed, sky torn, but then you will be above ground, so spread now is lodging that leads to Gardens held in honour where spreading is the vast kingdom and bliss, not the raging Flame that rips scalps where no spreading helps.\n\nMore: As for the earth, He spread it out as well also follows sky building to show sky and earth both prepared, as with the earth a lodging for the living and the dead, the earth smoothed like a bed, pegs, pairs, sleep, night, day, seven heavens, lamp, rain, grain, orchards, appointed Day. Spread, or “dahaha,” is to spread, to smooth, to extend, like the garden’s spread, the Pen’s spread, and the Reminder spread to the whole world. As well indicates after building sky, He spread earth, showing the same Builder does both, the One - asked which is harder, you or the sky, He built it—can restore. Use spread now to walk its spacious pathways to seek forgiveness, gardens, rivers, and to call openly and privately as Noah did, day and night, so spreading becomes your Gardens’ vast kingdom, not the raging Flame.",
  "31": "\"bringing forth its water and pastures,\" details what spreading earth produces, after “As for the earth, He spread it out.” The provision emerges.\n\n“bringing forth its water and pastures” translates “akhraja minha ma’aha wa mar‘aha,” He brought forth from it its water and its pasture, as with sending down from rainclouds pouring water, producing grain and plants and dense orchards, fresh water to drink, and given you fresh water to drink with mountains, and the spring flowing at will, Salsabil, and the flowing water if yours sinks who could bring it. Water and pastures are sustenance, like the earth smoothed to move and eat provision and the garden owners’ garden, now with water.\n\nThis follows spread earth to show that spread is not barren, but productive, as with the heavens’ lamp, and the appointed Day’s trumpet and crowds.\n\nBringing forth its water and pastures shows the Most Compassionate who holds birds, smooths earth, and descends command between seven heavens.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to credit water and pastures to the earth alone, like the despicable withholder who says ancient fables because blessed with wealth. Water and pastures are from the Lord of heavens and earth, Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak to Him.\n\nFor the human created from humble fluid until appointed time, bringing forth water and pastures should make you give rightful share to the asker and deprived, let the poor enter your garden today, say “Allah willing,” and be among the righteous who will have salvation, Gardens, vineyards, maidens, full cups, never hearing idle talk, generous gift, where water is the pure drink from your Lord and pastures are the Gardens where you recline, not the choking food and painful punishment in store for luxury deniers.\n\nExtra: Bringing forth its water and pastures also details what spreading earth produces, after “As for the earth, He spread it out.” Water and pastures are sustenance, as with sending down from rainclouds pouring water, producing grain and plants and dense orchards, fresh water to drink, and given you fresh water to drink with mountains, and the spring flowing at will, Salsabil, and the flowing water if yours sinks who could bring it. Water and pastures are provision for you and your animals, the grain and plants and dense orchards. Bringing forth shows the Most Compassionate who holds birds, smooths earth, and descends command between seven heavens. For the human created from humble fluid until appointed time, bringing forth water and pastures should make you give rightful share to the asker and deprived, let the poor enter your garden today, say “Allah willing,” and be among the righteous who will have salvation, Gardens, vineyards, where water is pure drink and pastures are Gardens where you recline.",
  "32": "\"and setting the mountains firmly ˹upon it˺—\" adds the pegs after water and pastures, completing earth preparation after sky building.\n\n“and setting the mountains firmly upon it—” translates “wa al-jibala arsaha,” and the mountains He anchored, as with the mountains as pegs, towering firm mountains and fresh water to drink, and placed firm mountains and given fresh water, the mountains blown away becoming mirage, tufts of wool, dunes of shifting sand, earth and mountains lifted and crushed. Setting firmly, or “arsaha,” is to anchor, like the Pen, the Record well-preserved, and the appointed time.\n\nMountains firmly set stabilize the lodging earth, the smoothed bed, the spacious pathways to walk, so you can move about regions and eat provision and to Him is resurrection, not swallowing as it quakes violently for those who feel secure.\n\nThis follows water and pastures to show that earth is both soft for growth and firm for stability, as with the seven heavens raised flawlessly and then dimmed night and brought forth daylight.\n\nA dash indicates more follows, “all as sustenance for you and your animals,” showing purpose, as with Gardens under rivers for ever and ever, excellent provision, and the vast kingdom.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think mountains firmly set means they will never be blown away, contradicting the appointed Day’s Trumpet. Firm is for the term until the Day of Final Decision is an appointed time.\n\nFor the living who were nothing, drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown, grateful or ungrateful, firm mountains should make you take the lead for the warning to humankind, every soul detained except the people of the right, so firm becomes your Gardens held in honour where firm is the elevated Garden’s canopy, not the Fire’s scorching skin, and sustenance now becomes the Gardens’ any fruit they desire, told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers.",
  "33": "\"all as ˹a means of˺ sustenance for you and your animals.\" gives the purpose of sky built, night dimmed, earth spread, water and pastures brought forth, mountains set. The purpose is provision.\n\n“all as a means of sustenance for you and your animals” translates “mata‘an lakum wa li-an‘amikum,” enjoyment for you and your cattle, as with the grain and plants and dense orchards produced by pouring water, the gardens and rivers supplied after seeking forgiveness, the wealth and children, the Gardens under rivers, the splendid homes, the excellent provision, and the vast kingdom and bliss. Sustenance, or “mata‘,” is provision, like the good sent forth found with Allah better and greater, the provision from sources never imagined, and the provision that will be brought if water sinks.\n\nFor you and your animals indicates that provision is for all dependents, not just the powerful, like the rightful share for the asker and deprived, the poor, orphan, captive.\n\nThis follows setting mountains firmly to show that all the building, from sky’s raising to mountains’ anchoring, is for sustenance.\n\nAll as sustenance follows the question which is harder, you or the sky, He built it, to show that harder creation is done for sustenance, so resurrection to bring to account for sustenance is easy.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use sustenance for indulging and amusing until they face their Day, loving fleeting and neglecting weighty Day ahead, withholding when touched with good.\n\nFor the one who asks “Is such a Creator unable to bring the dead back to life?” sustenance should answer, the One who built sky, dimmed night, spread earth, brought forth water and pastures, set mountains, all as sustenance, can certainly bring the dead back, as He is Most Capable to restore fingertips. Use sustenance now to be among the grateful who give food despite desire, so sustenance becomes your salvation, Gardens, vineyards, maidens, full cups, no idle talk, generous gift, where sustenance is eat and drink happily for what you used to do.",
  "34": "\"But, when the Supreme Disaster comes to pass—\" shifts from sustenance to the Supreme Disaster after sky, earth, and sustenance. The Disaster is the Day of Decision’s climax.\n\n“But, when the Supreme Disaster comes to pass—” translates “fa-idha ja’ati al-tammatu al-kubra,” when the great overwhelming calamity comes, the Tammat, the supreme, greatest, as with the Inevitable Hour, the Striking Disaster, the Day of Decision, the Day the Inevitable has come to pass, the Trumpet blown, the difficult Day far from easy, and Hell is one of the mightiest catastrophes. Supreme Disaster is the same as the mighty catastrophe, the raging Flame, the Fire that does not let live.\n\nThis follows sustenance for you and your animals to show that sustenance’s purpose is tested by the Disaster, as with death and life created to test which is best in deeds, with abundant rain as test, and wealth and children as test.\n\nWhen indicates the appointed time that can never be delayed, the Day they indulge until they face, the Day all will end up before your Lord, the mustaqarr.\n\nSupreme Disaster comes to pass, or “ja’ati,” is to arrive, as with the garden’s visitor while they slept and the horror cast on fortified hearts, but now supreme.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat Supreme Disaster as distant, like they see it as far. We see it as near, as with the Pen and the mountain.\n\nFor the living who were told to make room, give rightful share, and walk upright, Supreme Disaster should move you to take the path leading back to your Lord, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so Supreme Disaster finds you among the righteous who will have salvation, Gardens, vineyards, maidens, full cups, never hearing idle talk, where the Disaster is a warning taken, not a disaster entered, and the sustenance for you and your animals becomes the Gardens’ bliss and vast kingdom.",
  "35": "\"the Day every person will remember all ˹their˺ striving,\" describes the Supreme Disaster’s interior effect after it comes to pass. Memory awakens.\n\n“the Day every person will remember all their striving” translates “yawma yatadhakkaru al-insanu ma sa‘a,” the Day man will remember what he strove for, as with “All will then be informed of what they have sent forth and left behind, despite excuses the soul testifies against itself, the Pen writes, and you will be presented with no secret hidden. Remembering all striving contrasts with the halu‘ withholding when touched with good, distressed when touched with evil, except those who pray, and the garden owners who remembered “Glory be to our Lord, we have been wrongdoers” after deprivation.\n\nStriving, or “sa‘a,” is the same striving appreciated, “All this is surely a reward, your striving has been appreciated,” but here for the denier it is the striving that was indulging and amusing until they faced their Day, loving fleeting, neglecting weighty Day ahead, and persisting in denial until inevitable came, not praying, not feeding poor, indulging, denying.\n\nEvery person will remember indicates the self-reproaching soul’s testimony, the soul testifying against itself, the presentation where no secret stays hidden, and the informing.\n\nThis follows Supreme Disaster to show that the Disaster’s first impact is remembrance, and precedes Hellfire displayed for all to see, showing that remembrance is followed by seeing.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think remembering suffices after, like the deniers who will say “We were not of those who prayed” but remain in Hell where intercession does not benefit.\n\nFor the living, remember now, let every soul look to what it has sent forth for tomorrow and fear Allah, He is All-Aware of what you do, being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, and being saved from selfishness, so remembering on that Day is not woe and more woe, but “Read my record, I knew I would face my reckoning” in a life of bliss, in an elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, and you are told to eat and drink joyfully.",
  "36": "\"and the Hellfire will be displayed for all to see—\" gives the external sight that follows inner remembrance on the Supreme Disaster’s Day. The Hellfire is made visible.\n\n“and the Hellfire will be displayed for all to see—” translates “wa burrizati al-jahimu li-man yara,” the Blaze is brought out, made apparent for those who see, as with the Fire that almost bursts, raging Flame ripping scalps, Saqar not letting live, scorching skin, nineteen keepers, mighty catastrophe warning to humankind, and the difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers. Displayed for all to see means the Hellfire, lying in ambush as home for transgressors where they remain endless ages with no coolness except boiling water and pus, fitting reward, is now exposed, like the garden reduced to ashes while they slept exposed, and the horror cast on fortified hearts exposed.\n\nDisplayed, or “burrizat,” is to be brought forward, as with you will be presented with no secrets hidden, and the Throne borne by eight with angels on sides of the torn sky.\n\nFor all to see indicates that the Day every person will remember their striving is paired with seeing the Fire, so inner remembrance and outer sight align, unlike the deniers who woe on that Day to deniers, woe again, woe again.\n\nThis follows every person will remember all their striving to show that striving remembered is then seen as Fire for the wicked, and precedes the division, as for the one who transgressed and preferred fleeting world vs the one who was in awe and restrained.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think displayed for all to see means all enter. Displayed is to see, as with the wall with gate between grace and torment, where one side is grace and the other torment, and the righteous in Gardens will see the Fire from afar but are delivered from its horror with radiance and joy.\n\nFor the one who was nothing, drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown, grateful or ungrateful, Hellfire displayed should make you take the Right Way while you will, recite what is easy, and be among the righteous who will have salvation, Gardens, vineyards, maidens, full cups, never hearing idle talk, where displayed for all to see is not your home, but the Gardens displayed for all to see as bliss and vast kingdom, where the striving appreciated is yours.",
  "37": "\"then as for those who transgressed,\" begins the division after the Supreme Disaster, remembering and Hellfire displayed. The verse identifies the first group.\n\n“then as for those who transgressed” translates “fa-amma man tagha,” as for whoever exceeded bounds, like Pharaoh who transgressed all bounds, the despicable withholder, transgressor, evildoer, brute, those who felt secure that One in heaven will not swallow, who denied and disobeyed, turned his back striving, summoned and said “I am your lord, most high,” and like the luxury deniers, the garden owners fixated, and those who love fleeting and neglect weighty Day ahead. Tagha is to exceed, as with the blast exceeding, the wind exceeding, and the Fire not letting live.\n\nTransgressed here is paired next with “and preferred the fleeting life of this world,” showing that transgression is measured by preference.\n\nThis follows the Hellfire displayed for all to see to show who will enter it.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit transgression to Pharaoh. Transgression includes any who prefer fleeting, as with wealth and children diverting, merchandise better than what is with Allah, and honour that expels inferior.\n\nFor the one created from humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, earth lodging, mountains pegs, pairs, sleep, night, day, heavens, lamp, rain, grain, orchards, appointed Day, those who transgress choose the fleeting, so Hell will certainly be their home, as next states, while those in awe and restrained find Paradise. To be among the latter, be among those who fulfil vows, give food despite desire, and fear the horribly distressful Day, so transgression is not yours.\n\nMore: Then as for those who transgressed also includes the withholder of good who said ancient fables because blessed with wealth, the garden owners fixated, and the challenger demanding punishment. Transgressed, or “tagha,” is to exceed, as with the blast exceeding, the wind exceeding, and the Fire not letting live. To be among the transgressed’s Hell is to have preferred fleeting, so Hell will certainly be your home, where you remain for endless ages with no coolness and boiling pus. To avoid, be among those who were in awe of standing before their Lord and restrained desires, whose Paradise will certainly be their home, where transgression is replaced by restraint and preferred fleeting is replaced by preferred Hereafter, where Paradise is home, not Hell.",
  "38": "\"and preferred the ˹fleeting˺ life of this world,\" gives the second trait of the transgressors after exceeding bounds. The preference defines the transgression.\n\n“and preferred the fleeting life of this world” translates “wa athara al-hayata al-dunya,” he gave priority, chose, the near, worldly life, as with loving this fleeting world, totally neglecting a weighty Day ahead, indulging and amusing until they face their Day, loving merchandise better than what is with Allah, and honour that expels inferior. Preferred the fleeting indicates the transgressor’s scale, where the near, the ‘ajilah, outweighs the weighty, thaqila, the Gardens, the Bliss, and the vast kingdom.\n\nFleeting life of this world is the play, amusement, luxury, boasting, competition that becomes chaff, the garden’s early harvest without “Allah willing,” the caravan’s fanfare that drew crowds from the pulpit.\n\nThis follows transgressed to show that transgression is choosing fleeting, and precedes Hellfire will certainly be their home, showing consequence, and contrasts with “as for those who were in awe of standing before their Lord and restrained themselves from evil desires, Paradise will certainly be their home.”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think preferring fleeting means enjoying lawful provision like grain, plants, dense orchards, water and pastures, mountains firmly set as sustenance for you and your animals. Those are sustenance to use, not to prefer over the path leading back to your Lord.\n\nFor the living who were nothing, drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown, grateful or ungrateful, preferring fleeting is the ungrateful choice, whose chains, shackles, blazing Fire are prepared. Prefer the Hereafter, remember the Name morning and evening, prostrate part of night, glorify long, so fleeting is not preferred, but the Hereafter is, and the Hellfire that is in ambush as home for transgressors is not your home, but Paradise is.\n\nFurther: Preferred the fleeting life also explains why the luxur luxury deniers indulged and amused until they face their Day, loving merchandise better than what is with Allah, and honour that expels inferior. Preferred, or “athara,” is to choose, to give priority, as with loving fleeting and neglecting weighty Day ahead, hoarding and gathering, and feeling secure that One in heaven will not swallow. Fleeting life is the play, amusement, luxury, boasting, competition that becomes chaff, the garden’s early harvest without “Allah willing.” Preferred fleeting earns Hell as home, as with the five under: chains, shackles, blazing Fire. To prefer Hereafter, remember the Name morning and evening, prostrate part of night, glorify long, so fleeting is not preferred, but the Hereafter is, and the Hellfire that is in ambush as home for transgressors is not your home, but Paradise is, where you are held in honour and told to eat and drink happily.",
  "39": "\"the Hellfire will certainly be ˹their˺ home.\" states the recompense for transgressors who preferred the fleeting life. The home is Hell.\n\n“the Hellfire will certainly be their home” translates “fa-inna al-jahima hiya al-ma’wa,” then Hell is the refuge, the dwelling. Ma’wa is home, resort, as with Hell lying in ambush as home for transgressors where they remain endless ages, no coolness, boiling and pus, fitting reward, and the Fire that does not let live, scorching skin, nineteen keepers. Certainly, or emphatic, shows inevitability, as with the Inevitable Hour, the striking, the Day fifty thousand years, and the garden reduced to ashes while they slept.\n\nThis follows transgressed and preferred fleeting to show that preference leads to Hell as home, the same Hell displayed for all to see on the Supreme Disaster’s Day, when every person will remember striving.\n\nHell will certainly be their home follows the Supreme Disaster comes to pass, the Day every person remembers striving, and the Hellfire displayed.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think Hell as home is temporary lodging, like earth’s lodging for the living and the dead. Home here is ma’wa, final resort, where they remain for endless ages, not the lodging that is a test.\n\nFor the one who asks “Is there not a period when each human is nothing worth mentioning?” Hell as home warns not to prefer fleeting, but to be among those who were in awe of standing before their Lord and restrained evil desires, whose Paradise will certainly be their home, where they recline on canopied couches never seeing scorching heat, shade right above, fruit easy, silver vessels, crystal, ginger, Salsabil, eternal youths, bliss and kingdom, where home is Gardens held in honour, not Hell’s ambush.\n\nExtra: Hellfire will certainly be their home also warns that Hell as home is ma’wa, final resort, where they remain for endless ages, not the lodging earth for the living and the dead that is a test, but the endless lodging. Certainly emphasizes inevitability, as with the Inevitable Hour, the striking, the Day fifty thousand years, and the garden reduced to ashes while they slept. Hell will certainly be their home follows the Supreme Disaster comes to pass, the Day every person remembers striving, and the Hellfire displayed for all to see. To avoid, be among those who were nothing worth mentioning, then a drop for test, shown the Way, grateful, fulfilling vows, giving food despite desire, and fearing the distressful Day, so Hell as home is not yours, but Paradise will certainly be your home, where you recline on canopied couches never seeing scorching heat, shade right above, fruit easy, silver vessels, crystal, ginger, Salsabil, eternal youths, bliss and kingdom, where home is Gardens held in honour, not Hell’s ambush.",
  "40": "\"And as for those who were in awe of standing before their Lord and restrained themselves from ˹evil˺ desires,\" begins the counterpart to the transgressors, the saved group. The traits are awe and restraint.\n\n“And as for those who were in awe of standing before their Lord and restrained themselves from evil desires” translates “wa amma man khafa maqama rabbihi wa naha al-nafsa ‘ani al-hawa,” as for whoever feared the standing before his Lord and forbad the soul from caprice. Fear of standing, or “khafa maqama rabbihi,” is awe of the presentation where no secret stays hidden, the mustaqarr before your Lord, the Day every person will remember striving and Hellfire displayed, the Trumpet, the difficult Day, and the imminent punishment where every person will see what hands did and the disbeliever wishes to be dust. It is the same awe as those in awe without seeing with mighty reward, those who fear punishment knowing none should feel secure, and the wife of Pharaoh’s house near Allah.\n\nRestrained themselves from evil desires, or “naha al-nafsa ‘ani al-hawa,” is to forbid the soul from following caprice, whims, like halu‘ withholding when touched with good, gathering and hoarding that the Flame summons, desiring to deny what is yet to come, loving fleeting, neglecting weighty Day, and preferring fleeting.\n\nThis follows Hell will certainly be their home for transgressors to contrast, and precedes Paradise will certainly be their home, showing two homes for two choices.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to equate restraining desires with invented monasticism. Restraining here is from hawa, caprice that leads to shirk, not from lawful sustenance like grain, plants, dense orchards for you and your animals.\n\nFor the human created from mixed drop to test, made hear and see, shown the Way grateful or ungrateful, being in awe of standing before your Lord and restraining desires chooses grateful, so Paradise will certainly be your home, where restraint in days gone by becomes eating and drinking happily, radiance and joy, Garden and silk, where restraint is rewarded, not regretted.",
  "41": "\"Paradise will certainly be ˹their˺ home.\" states the reward for those in awe and restrained. Paradise is the counterpart to Hell as home for the other.\n\n“Paradise will certainly be their home” translates “fa-inna al-jannata hiya al-ma’wa,” then Paradise is the refuge. Ma’wa here is the same word as for Hell, but for the opposite group, showing two ma’was, two homes, as with residents of Fire not equal residents of Paradise, only the latter are truly successful, and submitted not wicked, and the Day of Decision’s woe vs salvation. Certainly again emphasizes inevitability, as with the Inevitable, the Striking, and the appointed time that never delays.\n\nParadise, or “jannah,” is the Gardens, vineyards, full-bosomed maidens, full cups, no idle talk, generous gift, the cool shade and springs for the righteous, any fruit they desire, told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers, the Gardens held in honour where the excepted who pray will be, the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach, and the Gardens under rivers for ever and ever.\n\nThis follows being in awe and restrained to show that awe and restraint lead to Paradise as home, the same Paradise the wife of Pharaoh prayed for, a house near Allah, delivered from Pharaoh and wrongdoing people.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think Paradise as home is merely reward for asceticism, like invented monasticism. It is reward for awe and restraint that includes fulfilling vows, feeding despite desire, giving rightful share, protecting families, and walking upright, all within lawful sustenance for you and your animals.\n\nFor the one created from humble fluid placed in secure place until appointed time, perfectly ordained, earth lodging, mountains pegs, Paradise as home is the elevation above the lodging, where sustenance for you and your animals becomes the Gardens’ bliss and vast kingdom, where the Supreme Disaster’s remembering and displaying become your deliverance with radiance and joy.",
  "42": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding the Hour, “When will it be?”\" reports the question asked to the Prophet after dividing transgressors preferring fleeting whose home is Hell and the aweful restrained whose home is Paradise. The question is about timing.\n\n“They ask you O Prophet regarding the Hour, ‘When will it be?’” translates the same question as “They ask you regarding the Hour, when will it be?” asked mockingly “When is the Day of Judgment?” and “When will this threat come if you are truthful?” and the challenger demanding punishment bound to come. Hour, or “sa‘ah,” is the Inevitable Hour, the Striking, the Day of Decision, the Supreme Disaster, the Day fifty thousand years, the difficult Day far from easy, the Day children’s hair turns grey, the Day the soul reaches collar bones, and the Day every person will see what hands did.\n\nThis follows Paradise will certainly be their home to show that after describing homes, timing becomes the next curiosity, as with the garden owners asking about harvest time and the hypocrites asking about honour.\n\nAsking “When will it be?” is not sincere inquiry like “which powerless force will help?” but the same mocking that thought stars put out and sky torn were far, but We see as near.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to answer with a date, as with copying the Record in the unseen.\n\nFor the Prophet told “Your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe of it” and “That knowledge rests with your Lord alone,” the answer is not to give a date but to warn. For the believer who is in awe of standing before his Lord, “When” is less important than being among those who restrain desires, so the Hour whether near or distant finds you with Paradise as home, not Hell, and with the Day every person remembers striving as your striving appreciated.",
  "43": "\"But it is not for you to tell its time.\" answers the Hour question after reporting they ask “When will it be?” The response limits the Prophet’s knowledge.\n\n“But it is not for you to tell its time” translates “fi-ma anta min dhikraha,” what are you concerning its remembrance, what have you to do with mentioning its time, as with “That knowledge rests with your Lord alone, your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe of it,” and “I do not know if what you are promised is near or my Lord has set a distant time, He is Knower of the unseen, disclosing none except to chosen messengers with angel-guards before and behind to ensure they fully deliver though He already knows and keeps account of everything.” The Prophet, though a noble Messenger not insane, of outstanding character, with never-ending reward, is not given the Hour’s time, just as the Pen writes but does not disclose unseen to all.\n\nNot for you to tell its time indicates that even the warner who knows the story of Moses, the quaking Blast, the second Blast, and the Supreme Disaster, does not know the Hour’s moment.\n\nThis follows they ask regarding the Hour to show that asking is not for him to answer with a date, and precedes “That knowledge rests with your Lord alone.”\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this diminishes the Prophet, as with how can humans be guides? Not telling time is part of being a warner, not a fortune-teller whose mumbling the Quran is not.\n\nFor the living who love fleeting and neglect weighty Day, not for you to tell its time should redirect you from asking “When?” mockingly to asking “How to prepare?” Prepare by being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, being true to trusts, so the Hour, whenever it is, finds you among the aweful restrained whose Paradise is home, not the transgressors whose Hell is home, where the Supreme Disaster’s remembering and displaying become your deliverance.",
  "44": "\"That knowledge rests with your Lord ˹alone˺.\" states where Hour knowledge belongs after saying it is not for you to tell its time. The knowledge is divine.\n\n“That knowledge rests with your Lord alone” translates “ila rabbika muntahaha,” to your Lord is its finality, its end, as with “That knowledge is with Allah alone, and I am only a clear warner,” and “He is the Knower of the unseen, disclosing none except to chosen messengers with angel-guards.” Knowledge of the Hour, like knowledge of whether you speak secretly or openly and what is hidden in the heart, and the seen and unseen, and the appointed time that never delays, is with the Most Compassionate, the Subtle, All-Aware, the All-Knowing, All-Wise who determines day and night, writes every calamity or blessing before creation, and keeps account of everything.\n\nRests with your Lord alone indicates the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the Most Compassionate, no one will dare speak to Him, the Blessed in Whose Hands rests all authority, Most Capable, who raised the sky high and formed it flawlessly, dimmed night, brought daylight, spread earth, brought forth water and pastures, set mountains, all as sustenance.\n\nThis follows not for you to tell its time to direct the questioner to the Lord, and precedes your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe, showing that the Prophet’s role is warning, not calendar.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as withholding mercy, like the withholder of good. Withholding Hour’s time is mercy that keeps you striving until inevitable comes, as with the garden’s visitor while they slept and the horror cast from where they never expected.\n\nFor the one created from humble fluid, that knowledge rests with your Lord should make you in awe of standing before Him and restrain hawa, so knowledge not given to you does not mean you are left without purpose, but that you are tested which is best in deeds, so you become among the bright faces looking at their Lord on the Day you will be driven, not the gloomy anticipating devastation.",
  "45": "\"Your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe of it.\" defines the Prophet’s task after stating Hour knowledge rests with his Lord alone. The duty is limited to warning.\n\n“Your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe of it” translates “innama anta mundhiru man yakhshaha,” you are only a warner for whoever fears it, is in awe of it, as with “We sent Noah to warn before painful punishment,” the warner who came and they said “You are extremely astray,” the keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” and the reminder to the whole world, the Pen, and the weighty revelation. In awe of it indicates the same awe as those in awe without seeing with mighty reward, those in awe of standing before their Lord who restrain desires and whose Paradise is home, and the lesson for whoever stands in awe after Pharaoh’s exemplary overtaking.\n\nOnly to warn shows the Messenger’s duty is only to convey clearly, not to protect from Allah if he were to disobey, not to have harm or benefit, but to deliver, as with “My duty is only to convey from Allah and deliver His messages,” and “Your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe.” Whoever is in awe will benefit, as with the blind man who came eager, being in awe, and the one indifferent who was given attention though not to blame if not purified.\n\nThis follows knowledge rests with your Lord to show that warning is the Prophet’s part, knowledge is the Lord’s.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit warning to a subset, as if others need not be warned. Warn whoever is in awe means that those in awe are the ones who will benefit, not that others are not warned, as with the Reminder for humankind, to whichever chooses to take the lead or lag.\n\nFor the living who were told to arise and warn, your duty is also to warn, not to know the Hour, so warn with reverence, purification, shunning idols, and perseverance, so those in awe become many, and the Day they see it as if they stayed no more than one evening or its morning does not catch them as transgressors preferring fleeting, but as Paradise homed, bright, and delivered from horror with radiance and joy.",
  "46": "\"On the Day they see it, it will be as if they had stayed ˹in the world˺ no more than one evening or its morning.\" describes the perception of time on the Day they see the Hour. The duration shrinks.\n\n“On the Day they see it, it will be as if they had stayed in the world no more than one evening or its morning” translates the feeling when the Supreme Disaster comes, when every person remembers striving and Hellfire is displayed, when they are told this is the Day of Final Decision gathered with earlier, when they are asked about scheme, when they see what they had been warned of. Evening or its morning, or “‘ashiyatan aw duhaha,” an evening or its forenoon, indicates a very short stay, like the garden’s one night while they slept and it was reduced to ashes, the seven nights and eight days for ‘Ad, and the period when each human is nothing worth mentioning, a period of time.\n\nAs if they had stayed no more than one evening or its morning shows that the long life that made them love fleeting, neglect weighty Day, indulge and amuse until they faced their Day, hoard and withhold, and feel secure that One in heaven will not swallow, retrospectively feels brief. The weighty Day ahead of them that they neglected makes the fleeting world seem fleeting indeed, like the play, amusement, luxury, boasting, competition that becomes chaff.\n\nThis follows your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe to show what the warned will perceive, and closes the surah after being in awe and Paradise as home.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to take the short perception as proof that worldly life is insignificant, so striving is pointless, as with those who think they will be left without purpose. Short perception makes striving more urgent, not less, as with “let every soul look to what it has sent forth for tomorrow.”\n\nFor the one who was nothing, then a drop, hearing and seeing, Way shown, grateful or ungrateful, perceiving stay as one evening should make you choose the Right Way now, recite what is easy, establish prayer, pay alms, lend good loan, and seek forgiveness, so the one evening feels as if it were spent as Gardens held in honour where you are told to eat and drink happily, rewarded as good-doers, not as Hell in ambush where you remain for endless ages with no coolness and boiling pus, a fitting reward."
};
